    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2710.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 710,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several strange and unique properties that make it unlike almost any other substance on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rivers), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily carry minerals, nutrients, and chemicals through our bodies and the ecosystem.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life, and life on Earth would likely never have evolved.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This allows the oceans to regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **Scarcity:** Despite how abundant it looks, about **96.5% of Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in rivers, lakes, and shallow aquifers.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop called the water cycle (or hydrological cycle). It evaporates from the oceans into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers and groundwater back to the ocean. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. Water and Life\n* **Biology:** Water is the medium of life. The human body is made up of about **60% water**, and the brain is roughly 73% water. Cells are filled with water-based fluid, allowing the chemical reactions of life to take place.\n* **Evolution:** Scientists believe life began in the Earth's oceans over 3 billion years ago. Without liquid water, metabolic processes cannot happen, which is why the search for alien life in the universe always begins with the search for water.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscape, drives our weather and climate, makes up the majority of our bodies, and is the essential ingredient that makes life possible."
